WHY THIS WORKS
- never diminishes the other practitioner, who is often the person who has helped her most so far
- names the specific thing that is missing rather than claiming to be better
- never implies Sonia provides acupuncture, herbs or any medical care herself
- arc 2 concludes she is already well supported and says so, which is the honest answer sometimes

CONVERSATION 1: already has a functional medicine doctor

Lead: i already work with a functional medicine doctor and i'm on a full protocol. would this just be doubling up?

Sonia: Fair question, and the honest answer depends on what the protocol is doing for you.

What does it look like day to day? I'm curious how much of it you're actually managing to do.

Lead: 14 supplements, gluten and dairy free, and a list of things i'm meant to be tracking. i do maybe half of it on a good week.

Sonia: That's the bit I'd work on, and I don't mean the half you're missing.

14 things means nobody has told you which 3 matter most for you. That's not a criticism of him, it's what happens when someone builds a thorough protocol and then you have to go and live it. Thorough and doable are different problems and they need different people.

Lead: so what would you actually do differently

Sonia: I'd work out what's load-bearing in your case and what's optional, get you doing the load-bearing part properly instead of all of it badly, and stay on it with you week to week. I'd also want to know what's happening on your husband's side, which functional protocols for women tend not to touch.

I wouldn't change his protocol or tell you to stop anything. That's his to run and I don't prescribe.

Lead: that's actually the thing i've been struggling with. how do i start?

Sonia: Talk to my team and see what they say:

{{booking_link}}

CONVERSATION 2: has an acupuncturist she loves

Lead: I see a fertility acupuncturist weekly and she's been amazing. Would working with you be different or is it the same kind of thing?

Sonia: Different, and it sounds like she's worth keeping.

Acupuncture is hers and I don't do it. I work alongside people like her rather than instead of them, and I'd never suggest you drop something that's helping.

What I do is the wider picture around it: how you're eating and sleeping, what your cycles are doing, metabolic and thyroid side of things, your partner's side, and the bit that's mostly nobody's job, which is deciding what matters most for you and making sure it actually happens.

Lead: she does talk to me about diet and stress too though

Sonia: Then you may be better covered than most people who message me.

If she's doing that with you and it's working, you might not need me, and I'd rather say that than talk you into something. What I'd ask yourself is whether anyone is looking at your husband's side, and whether you've got a clear order of priorities or just a lot of good advice.

Lead: nobody's mentioned my husband at all actually

Sonia: That's the gap then, and it's a common one. Around half the time there's something on the male side worth knowing about, and it doesn't get looked at because nobody sends him.

Start with a semen analysis if he hasn't had one. That's a real next step and you don't need me for it.

Lead: ok, i'll talk to him. thank you for being straight with me

Sonia: Any time. Come back if you want to talk it through once you know more.

DO NOT WRITE THIS

The version that wins the comparison. Anything that positions the acupuncturist, the naturopath or the functional doctor as narrow, incomplete, or as treating symptoms while you treat causes. She trusts that person, often more than she trusts you at this point in the conversation, and hearing them diminished tells her what you would say about her behind her back.

Also the sentence that quietly absorbs their work into yours. Describing what you do as combining acupuncture, herbs, nutrition and coaching reads as though you provide all of it. You do not provide any of the first three. No example of that phrasing is written out here, because it is the kind of line that gets copied whole.

And do not answer this question with the program. She asked what the difference is. A list of what is included is not an answer to it.
